// M3d — CPU Statement Parallelization
//
// M3d ships no new compile-error classes.  CPU parallelization is fully automatic:
// the compiler decides what to parallelize and what to run sequentially.  There is
// no opt-in syntax that a user can write incorrectly, and no new type rules that
// produce new diagnostics.
//
// The feature that IS detectable at compile time — using `wait` on a CPU-bound
// callee (which silently defeats parallelization by acting as an ordering barrier)
// — was already covered by the WaitOnNonMayBlockWarning diagnostic introduced in
// M2 and exercised in v0_3_m2_errors.ynz.  That diagnostic now explains the
// ordering-barrier consequence in its why-text, which is the only M3d change to
// existing diagnostics.
//
// See design/concurrency.md "CPU Statement Parallelization (M3d)" for the
// promotion rules, decline rules, and design divergences.
